    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d332">

                <pb facs="0368" n="270" xml:id="pg270"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/5371</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-07-21">July 21, 1930</date>.<lb/>[Received August
                        27.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 333</seg></opener>
                <p>Sir: With reference to the Legation’s telegrams No. 519 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >509</hi>] of June 27, 3 p.m., and No. 559 of July 10, 3 p.m.,<anchor n="88"
                        xml:id="d332-f1"/> concerning the Tientsin customs, I have the honor to
                    enclose herewith a copy of Diplomatic Circular No. 65, of July 18, 1930, quoting
                    the text of the telegram addressed by Marshal Yen Hsi-shan to his representative
                    in Peiping. This telegram is apparently intended as a reply to the note
                    despatched by the Senior Minister to Marshal Yen, the text of which is given in
                    the Legation’s first telegram under reference.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mahlon F.
                            Perkins</hi></signed><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Counselor of
                        Legation</hi></closer>


                <xi:include href="d332-a1.xml" xpointer="d332-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
